================================================================================
ADO.NET CLASS UPDATES LOG
Microsoft SQL Server to PostgreSQL Migration
Date: 2026-01-26
================================================================================

This log documents the replacement of SQL Server-specific ADO.NET classes with
their Npgsql (PostgreSQL) equivalents throughout the codebase.

================================================================================
FILE: ProductRepository.cs
================================================================================
Location: sourceCode/DataAccess/ProductRepository.cs
Total Methods Updated: 10+ methods (all database operations)

USING STATEMENT UPDATES:
-------------------------
REMOVED: using Microsoft.Data.SqlClient;
ADDED:   using Npgsql;

CLASS REPLACEMENTS:
-------------------

1. Connection Class:
   OLD: SqlConnection
   NEW: NpgsqlConnection
   Occurrences: 3
   Locations:
   - Field declaration: private NpgsqlConnection _connection;
   - GetConnectionAsync return type: Task<NpgsqlConnection>
   - GetConnectionAsync instantiation: new NpgsqlConnection(_connectionString)

2. Command Class:
   OLD: SqlCommand
   NEW: NpgsqlCommand
   Occurrences: 7
   Methods Updated:
   - GetAllProductsAsync: new NpgsqlCommand(sql, connection)
   - GetProductByIdAsync: new NpgsqlCommand(sql, connection)
   - InsertProductAsync: Multiple NpgsqlCommand instances in transaction
   - UpdateProductAsync: new NpgsqlCommand(sql, connection)
   - DeleteProductAsync: new NpgsqlCommand(sql, connection)
   - GetProductsByPriceRangeAsync: new NpgsqlCommand(sql, connection)
   - GetLowStockProductsAsync: new NpgsqlCommand(sql, connection)

3. DataReader Class:
   OLD: SqlDataReader
   NEW: NpgsqlDataReader
   Occurrences: 1
   Locations:
   - MapProductFromReader method signature: private static Product MapProductFromReader(NpgsqlDataReader reader)

PARAMETER HANDLING:
-------------------
No changes required:
- command.Parameters.AddWithValue() method is compatible with Npgsql
- Parameter names with @ prefix (@ProductId, @Name, etc.) are supported by Npgsql
- Parameter binding logic remains unchanged

TRANSACTION HANDLING:
---------------------
No changes required:
- BeginTransactionAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- CommitAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- RollbackAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- Transaction patterns remain unchanged

CONNECTION STATE HANDLING:
--------------------------
No changes required:
- ConnectionState enum is provider-agnostic (part of System.Data)
- OpenAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- CloseAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- DisposeAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- State checking (connection.State != ConnectionState.Open) remains unchanged

ASYNC METHODS:
--------------
No changes required:
- ExecuteReaderAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- ExecuteNonQueryAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- ExecuteScalarAsync() is compatible with Npgsql
- All async/await patterns remain unchanged

METHODS UPDATED (SUMMARY):
---------------------------
1. GetConnectionAsync: Changed return type and instantiation
2. GetAllProductsAsync: Changed command class
3. GetProductByIdAsync: Changed command class
4. InsertProductAsync: Changed command classes (multiple in transaction)
5. UpdateProductAsync: Changed command class
6. DeleteProductAsync: Changed command class
7. GetProductsByPriceRangeAsync: Changed command class
8. GetLowStockProductsAsync: Changed command class
9. ExecuteInTransactionAsync: No changes (uses generic patterns)
10. MapProductFromReader: Changed parameter type
11. DisposeAsync: No changes (uses generic patterns)

NO CHANGES IN OTHER FILES:
---------------------------
As expected per transformation plan:
- Business/ProductService.cs: No direct database access
- CLI classes: No direct database access
- Models/Product.cs: POCO class, no database dependencies
- Program.cs: No direct database access

COMPATIBILITY NOTES:
--------------------
1. Npgsql fully implements ADO.NET interfaces:
   - IDbConnection (NpgsqlConnection)
   - IDbCommand (NpgsqlCommand)
   - IDataReader (NpgsqlDataReader)
   
2. Method signatures are compatible:
   - Same parameter types
   - Same return types
   - Same async patterns

3. Behavior is equivalent:
   - Connection pooling works similarly
   - Parameter binding works the same way
   - Transaction handling follows same patterns
   - Reader iteration is identical

